+DTA & 70.8 & 67.6 & 67.3 & 28102 & 52.5\\
+CoPhy & 69.2 & 66.3 & 66.1 & 23066 & 13.8\\
+DB2Advis & 75.9 & 70.0 & 68.0 & 38 & 0.1\\
+Two-phase greedy & 72.4 & 67.3 & 67.1 & 2828 & 4.3\\
+\quad + Wii & 72.4 & 68.3 & 68.3 & 430 & 1.1\\
+\quad + Esc & 72.4 & 67.3 & 67.1 & 2184 & 4.9\\
+\quad + Wii + Esc & 72.4 & 68.3 & 68.3 & 430 & 0.9\\
+\midrule
+AutoAdmin (K=10) & \multicolumn{3}{c}{67.7} & 2671 & 3.9\\
+Drop (K=10) & \multicolumn{3}{c}{72.2} & 1066 & 2.5\\
+Dexter (10\%) & \multicolumn{3}{c}{70.7} & 0 & 2.4\\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\caption{Relative workload cost (\%, lower is better) at 5, 10 and 15\,GB storage budgets, and what-if calls and algorithm runtime at 10\,GB. The last block has no storage budget: AutoAdmin and Drop at $K=10$ indexes, Dexter at a 10\% saving threshold.}\label{tab:compare}
+\end{table}
+
+\begin{figure}[h]\centering
+\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_compare.png}
+\caption{Relative workload cost (left, from 1.5\,GB) and what-if calls (right) against the storage budget. Dashed: Wii and Esc on top of the two-phase tuner; Wii~+~Esc coincides with Wii.}\label{fig:compare}
+\end{figure}
+
+\textbf{At 10\,GB} CoPhy is best (66.3\%) but uses 23\,066 calls. The plain two-phase tuner is 1.0 point behind it (67.3\%) with 2\,828 calls, and level with Relaxation and DTA (67.6\%), AutoAdmin (67.7\%, $K=10$). With Wii the tuner is at 68.3\% with 430 calls: 2.0 points behind CoPhy, 0.7 behind Relaxation and DTA, with 1.9\% of CoPhy's calls, 23\% of Relaxation's and 1.5\% of DTA's. It beats Extend (70.5\%), DB2Advis (70.0\%), Drop (72.2\%) and Dexter (70.7\%). Only DB2Advis uses fewer calls (38) and it is 1.7 points worse than Wii. At 15\,GB the order is the same (CoPhy 66.1, two-phase 67.1, DTA 67.3, Relaxation 67.4, Wii 68.3). Wii is about one point worse than the plain tuner from 6.5\,GB on, as in the call-budget experiments.
+
+\textbf{At 5\,GB} the greedy variants (72.4\%) are worse than Extend, Relaxation and DTA (70.4--70.8\%) and than CoPhy (69.2\%). They are also worse than at 4.25\,GB (71.5\%, Wii 71.0\%): with more room the greedy search takes a different early index and ends up worse, which we did not investigate. Below about 4\,GB all algorithms except DB2Advis are within 2--3 points of each other.
+
+\textbf{Esc} changes the cost of the plain tuner by at most 0.2 points while saving up to 28\% of its calls (previous section), which puts it at 2\,184 calls at 10\,GB. Caveats: the tuner uses index width 2 and the original algorithms the study's own configuration; calls are counted by different code paths (the platform's cost-request counter for the original eight, the oracle's calls for ours); the original eight and our variants come from different runs; all costs are optimizer estimates.
+
+\section{Real execution}\label{sec:exec}
+Everything above uses optimizer estimates for hypothetical indexes. For the third objective of the proposal we built the indexes selected at a 5\,000\,MB budget for real in the SF10 database and ran the 19 queries (\texttt{scripts/execute\_selected\_indexes.py}; runtime per query is the median of the timed repetitions, the analysis output reports three; a query is stopped after 5 minutes and counted as 5 minutes). The workload without indexes takes 669.9\,s. Indexes are those chosen by the first repetition of each configuration.
+
+\begin{table}[h]\centering\small
+\begin{tabular}{lrrrl}
+\hline
+Configuration & Indexes & Estimated & Real & Queries $>10\%$ slower\\
+\hline
+Extend & 6 & 28.7\% & $-7.7$\% & 10\\
+Relaxation & 7 & 28.8\% & $-3.3$\% & 9\\
+Two-phase greedy & 7 & 26.5\% & $+35.1$\% & 7\\
+Two-phase greedy + Wii & 3 & 26.4\% & $-19.4$\% & 9\\
+Two-phase greedy + Esc & 6 & 26.5\% & $-40.0$\% & 11\\
+Two-phase greedy + Wii + Esc & 3 & 26.4\% & $-38.3$\% & 9\\
+\hline
+\end{tabular}
+\caption{Improvement of the workload runtime over no indexes (positive is faster), estimated against measured, at 5\,GB.}
+\end{table}
+
+\begin{figure}[h]\centering\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{fig_execution.png}\caption{Estimated against measured improvement; the dashed line is agreement.}\end{figure}
+
+The estimates do not predict the measured result. All six configurations are estimated to improve the workload by 26--29\%, but only the plain two-phase greedy is faster in practice; the other five make the workload slower. Two-phase greedy and two-phase greedy + Esc have the same estimated improvement (26.5\%) and measured improvements 75 points apart, with index sets of 7 and 6 indexes. Queries 9 and 10 are more than 10\% slower in all six configurations, and several others (1, 4, 7, 12) in most of them. The differences between methods in the estimated tables (a few points) are much smaller than the gap between estimate and execution, so those tables do not show which method chooses better indexes in practice.
+
+What this does not show: that Wii or Esc choose worse indexes in general. It is one budget, one selection per configuration, and 19 queries; Esc returns the configuration of its simulated greedy, built from derived costs, which can differ from the plain tuner's; timeouts are counted at 5 minutes, which hides the size of the slowdown of the slowest queries. We have not examined which indexes cause the slowdowns, nor tested possible causes (for example statistics after the indexes are built, cache state or planner settings), so we give no explanation.
+
+
+\section{Discussion and limitations}\label{sec:limits}
+\begin{itemize}
+\item \textbf{Estimates against execution.} Section~\ref{sec:exec} is the only part that measures real runtimes, at one budget (5\,GB) with one selection per configuration. It shows that estimated improvement does not predict measured improvement, so the quality figures of Sections~\ref{sec:repro}--\ref{sec:compare} describe the optimizer's model and nothing more. We did not find out which indexes cause the slowdowns, or why.
+\item \textbf{Statistics draws.} Each \texttt{ANALYZE} moves the improvement of a run by about 1 point (Wii 32.2--33.3\% over four draws). Absolute values should not be read to better than that; paired differences between methods on the same draw were stable. The assumption and gap experiments (Sections~\ref{sec:gaps} and~\ref{sec:assump}) use one draw, and the Esc results two or three.
+\item \textbf{Our own implementations.} Neither Wii nor Esc has public code, and we had no reference implementation to compare against. The paper's numbers are not reproduced: Esc saves up to 28\% of the calls here against about 60\% reported for SQL Server, and Wii's 86\% saving was measured on one workload. Of the Esc paper, only Esc-B is implemented; Esc-I, the rate-based filter and the MCTS variants are not.
+\item \textbf{Esc additions are ours.} The storage-aware bound and the clamp are not in the paper. The bound is valid by construction (checked by unit tests and on synthetic data). The clamp assumes monotone costs, which PostgreSQL does not guarantee (we saw $\eta_U<\eta_L$), so it is a heuristic. We did not measure on TPC-H how much of Esc's stopping the storage-aware bound is responsible for; the ablation configuration exists (\texttt{configs/tpch\_sf10\_esc\_ablation.json}) but its results are not part of this report.
+\item \textbf{Wii was never limited by its budget.} Wii used at most 437 calls, so the budgets of the sweep did not constrain it; a sweep at 50--400 calls, and Esc under a call budget, are open. The greedy variants dip at 5\,GB (72.4\% against 71.5\% at 4.25\,GB), which we did not investigate.
+\item \textbf{One workload, one DBMS.} Only TPC-H at SF10 on PostgreSQL; TPC-DS and JOB are not run. A teammate's run with \texttt{max\_parallel\_workers\_per\_gather}=8 gave a lower cost scale and, as reported to us, the same qualitative patterns; we report only the default-settings results.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\section{Conclusion}
+The reproduction supports the original study's main findings on PostgreSQL 18: quality is determined mainly by whether a few key indexes fit in the budget, most algorithms converge to similar estimated cost, and they differ mostly in run time and what-if calls. In estimated terms, Wii keeps most of the tuner's quality with a small fraction of its calls, and Esc saves calls under a storage budget once its bound accounts for storage, but it gives nothing on top of Wii. The real-execution check is a reminder that the estimates are the weak point: five of the six configurations we executed made the workload slower. The next steps that would matter most are executing more budgets and several selections per configuration, finding the indexes behind the slowdowns, the Wii sweep at small call budgets and the Esc ablation on TPC-H.
+
+\section*{Reproducibility}\begingroup\raggedright
+Repository \texttt{RameezWahid/magic-mirror-revisited}: reproduction on branch \texttt{sf10-arijit} (results in \texttt{benchmark\_results/tpch\_sf10\_paper\_pg18.7}, CoPhy width 2 in \texttt{tpch\_sf10\_cophy\_w2\_pg18.7}); two-phase greedy and Wii in \texttt{selection/budget\_aware.py} and \texttt{selection/algorithms/two\_phase\_greedy\_algorithm.py}; Esc on branch \texttt{esc-arijit} (\texttt{selection/esc.py}, \texttt{tests/test\_esc.py}, 10 unit tests); storage-limited runs with \texttt{configs/tpch\_sf10\_new\_algos.json} through \texttt{scripts/run\_repetitions.sh} and \texttt{scripts/analyze\_results.py}; real execution with \texttt{scripts/execute\_selected\_indexes.py} (results in \texttt{benchmark\_results/execution/tpch\_sf10\_pg18.6.csv}); the raw statistics-draw runs of Sections~\ref{sec:setup}--\ref{sec:esc} in \texttt{out/*/runs.jsonl}. Environment: PostgreSQL 18.7, HypoPG 1.4.5, Dexter 0.6.3, gurobipy 13, PuLP 3 (PuLP 4 is incompatible with the CoPhy code).
+
+\endgroup
+\section*{References}
+\begin{enumerate}\small
+\item J.\ Kossmann, S.\ Halfpap, M.\ Jankrift, R.\ Schlosser. Magic Mirror in My Hand, Which Is the Best in the Land? An Experimental Evaluation of Index Selection Algorithms. PVLDB 13(11), 2020.
+\item S.\ Halfpap et al. Looking Deeply into the Magic Mirror. PVLDB 17, 2024 (demonstration; platform used here).
+\item X.\ Wang, W.\ Wu, C.\ Wang, V.\ Narasayya, S.\ Chaudhuri. Wii: Dynamic Budget Reallocation In Index Tuning. SIGMOD 2024 (PACMMOD 2(3)).
+\item X.\ Wang, W.\ Wu, V.\ Narasayya, S.\ Chaudhuri. Esc: An Early-Stopping Checker for Budget-aware Index Tuning. PVLDB 18(5), 2025.
+\end{enumerate}
+\end{document}
